Run a command with a vault's variables in its environment.

The secrets are decrypted in memory and handed to the child process. Nothing is written to disk, nothing enters your shell history, and nothing appears in `ps` — values travel in the environment block, never in the argument vector.

This removes the plaintext file, not the master password. The vault key is wrapped under your master key, so decryption needs it wherever this runs; in CI, feed it with --password-stdin.

The command runs directly, not through a shell. For a pipeline or a shell builtin, ask for one: `-- sh -c 'a | b'`.

Masking does not cover values the command transforms, files it writes, core dumps, `/proc/<pid>/environ` (readable by the same user) or programs that log their own environment. It pipes stdout and stderr, so the command sees no terminal: colour and progress output turn off, and Python holds its output in a buffer, so set `PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1`. Without a terminal, `--password-stdin` is required.

Names that start a program or load code (`EDITOR`, `PAGER`, `GIT_ASKPASS`, `npm_config_*`, ...) are refused unless `--allow-unsafe-names`; proxy and certificate names only warn.

Once the command has started, evnx exits with the command's own exit code. The codes 10–15 listed in `evnx cloud --help` apply only to a failure before it starts (not signed in, server unreachable, …).

`--version` here means the vault version, so clap's auto-generated `--version` is disabled on this subcommand, as it is on `pull`. Use `evnx --version` for the program version.

Usage: evnx cloud run [OPTIONS] [-- <COMMAND>...]

Arguments:
  [COMMAND]...
          The command to run, after `--`.
          
          `last = true` makes the separator mandatory, so a flag meant for the child — `-- npm run build --prod` — cannot be mistaken for one of evnx's own.

Options:
      --vault <VAULT>
          Vault to read: `name`, `name/environment`, or an id.
          
          Optional in a directory bound with `evnx cloud link`.
          
          Repeatable: with several, one master-password unlock opens them all and they are merged left to right. A key in more than one of them is refused (the command does not run) unless `--allow-override` is given. `--version` belongs to one vault, so it is refused with more than one. `--include`/`--exclude` apply to the merged result.

      --allow-override
          With several `--vault`, let the later vault win a key both hold.
          
          Each overlap is named on stderr (key and winning vault, never a value). Without it an overlap stops the run. Does nothing with one vault.

      --version <N>
          Version to inject. Defaults to the latest

      --min-version <N>
          Refuse to run when a vault is below this version.
          
          Checked for each vault before the master password is asked for: a server that serves an old version (a rollback) must not quietly run a deploy with stale secrets. Exits 2 and the command does not run. With `--version V`, V below N is refused without any request. Also read from `EVNX_MIN_VERSION`, which the command does not inherit.
          
          [env: EVNX_MIN_VERSION=]

      --include <GLOB>
          Inject only variables whose name matches one of these globs.
          
          Example: --include "DB_*,AWS_*"
          
          Least privilege for a subprocess: a build step that needs only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` has no reason to be handed your database password.

      --exclude <GLOB>
          Skip variables whose name matches one of these globs.
          
          Example: --exclude "*_LOCAL,*_TEST"
          
          Applied after --include, so a name matching both is skipped.

      --password-stdin
          Read the master password from stdin instead of prompting.
          
          Only the first line is consumed; the child inherits the rest of stdin.

      --allow-unsafe-names
          Inject variables that make a program load or run other code.
          
          `PATH`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, `BASH_ENV`, `EDITOR`, `PAGER`, `GIT_ASKPASS`, `npm_config_*` and similar are refused by default: anyone who can push to the vault could use them to run code in your process. Pass this only if the vault is meant to set them.

      --mask
          Mask secret values in the command's output as `***`.
          
          On by default when output is not a terminal (a CI log, an agent), where what is printed is kept. Only values the classifier calls secret (by name or by value) are masked. It prevents accidents, not exfiltration: a command that transforms a value gets past it.

      --no-mask
          Never mask the command's output

      --prefer <SIDE>
          Which side wins when a variable is set both in your environment and in the vault: `vault` (default) or `env`.
          
          `vault` lets the vault replace what your environment holds (today's behaviour). `env` keeps a variable your environment already holds, even an empty one, and only fills the names it leaves unset: for testing with a local value over a shared one. A line on stderr names what was kept, never a value. Names are checked either way; a kept value is not masked, since evnx did not inject it.

          Possible values:
          - vault: The vault or env file wins: today's behaviour
          - env:   A variable already set in the environment wins (even when empty); the vault or file only fills names that are unset
          
          [default: vault]

      --server <URL>
          evnx server to talk to. Overrides EVNX_SERVER and config.toml.
          
          Plain http:// is refused for anything but a loopback address, because the access and refresh tokens travel in request headers.

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
   10  Not signed in, or the token was rejected
   11  Forbidden, or the email is not verified
   12  The plan's limit is reached
   13  Server unreachable, not an evnx server, or TLS roots unusable
   14  Conflict, or locked
   15  Server error, or rate limited
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)
    N  Once the command has started: its own exit code, or 128+n if signal n killed it
